  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nEarly afternoon surface observations show light snow lingering \nnear Beckley as a departing shortwave exits southeastward, while\nthe remainder of the lowlands across southeast Ohio, northeast \nKentucky, the Metro Valley, and the southern coalfields have \ndried out under broken to overcast stratocumulus ceilings. \nWest-northwest winds of 10 to 15 mph with gusts of 15 to 25 mph \nwill continue through this afternoon before gradually subsiding \nafter sunset. \n\nA persistent low-level moisture fetch off Lakes Michigan, Huron,\nand Erie will remain directed into the central Appalachians from\nthis afternoon through Sunday. Embedded within broader northwest\nflow aloft, a pair of upper-level shortwaves will cross the\nregion late tonight and again during the day Sunday, providing\nmodest synoptic forcing for ascent. Blended central guidance\nwas notably too low on precipitation probabilities, holding \nvalues below 10 percent across the lowlands and 10 to 25 percent\nin the mountains, and much too cold for tonight given overcast \nskies and ongoing boundary-layer mixing. Accordingly, \nprecipitation chances were raised across the forecast area late \ntonight through Sunday, and tonight's low temperatures were \nboosted several degrees above central guidance into the mid to\nupper 30s across the lowlands and the upper 20s to lower 30s in\nthe mountains. \n\nUpstream 12Z observed soundings at Wilmington OH (ILN) and \nPittsburgh PA (PIT), along with forecast soundings across \nCharleston, Huntington, Parkersburg, Athens, and Snowshoe, \nreveal that the moist boundary layer remains relatively shallow \nand capped by a subsidence inversion between H800 and H750.\nAcross the lowlands, temperatures at the top of the moist layer \nonly reach -4C to -9C, which is warmer than the dendritic \ngrowth zone and marginal for heterogeneous ice nucleation. As a\nresult, precipitation across the lowlands late tonight through \nSunday will fall primarily as spotty drizzle or very light rain,\nwith relatively elevated cloud bases around 2000 to 3000 ft \nlikely limiting how much drizzle survives to the surface. \n\nAcross the Northeast Mountains and higher ridges above roughly\n2500 to 3000 ft, boundary-layer temperatures remain subfreezing\n(-1C to -6C), supporting primarily light snow showers. Because\nthe capping inversion restricts moisture depth and keeps cloud\ntops largely below the dendritic growth zone, snow accumulations\nthrough Sunday evening will be light, generally a dusting to 1\ninch, though locally slightly higher amounts are possible\nbeneath the most persistent lake-enhanced streamers. \n\nHeading into Sunday night and Monday, surface high pressure\nstrengthens to around 1035 mb as it shifts east across the Ohio\nValley and central Appalachians. Low-level winds back toward \nthe north and northeast at 5 to 10 mph, cutting off the Great \nLakes moisture trajectory and allowing clouds to scatter out by \nMonday morning. Dry weather will then prevail through the \nremainder of the upcoming workweek under amplified mid-level \nridging. After chilly daytime highs in the 30s in the mountains \nand low to mid 40s in the lowlands on Sunday, temperatures will \nmoderate steadily next week, climbing into the upper 40s to near\n50 across the lowlands Monday, the 50s Tuesday and Wednesday, \nand the low to mid 60s by Thursday and Friday. Central guidance \nalso appeared too dry with afternoon mixed dew points for at \nleast early in the work week and have reduced these some. Clear \nskies, light winds, and dry air will still promote strong \nradiational cooling at night, dropping lows into the mid 20s to \nlower 30s Monday night and Tuesday night, coldest in sheltered \ncold-air drainage valleys away from major rivers, before \novernight lows moderate into the 30s and lower 40s late in the \nweek.",
